1
00:00:15,835 --> 00:00:19,978
[SPEAKER_01]: This is from the Anguttara Nikaya, a sutta called Walking.

2
00:00:20,979 --> 00:00:21,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Walking?

3
00:00:21,579 --> 00:00:21,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Walking.

4
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:42,815
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh, it just says, if a sensual thought or thought of ill-will or cruelty arises while walking, and if you tolerate it and do not abandon it, then you are devoid of effort and moral shame, constantly lazy and lacking energy while walking.

5
00:00:46,827 --> 00:00:58,076
[SPEAKER_01]: And it goes on, whether walking, standing, sitting or lying down, one who thinks bad thoughts, connected with household life, has entered upon the wrong path.

6
00:00:59,157 --> 00:01:04,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Infatuated by delusive things, such a person cannot be free.

7
00:01:04,861 --> 00:01:12,347
[SPEAKER_01]: But one who has his thoughts calmed and delights in stilling of thought, such a person can be free.

8
00:01:13,928 --> 00:01:19,673
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a lot of walking meditation out there, people like to do.

9
00:01:20,494 --> 00:01:24,678
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's... they focus on the feeling of their feet on the sand.

10
00:01:24,858 --> 00:01:25,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

11
00:01:25,439 --> 00:01:26,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the actual nature of the thinking.

12
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:27,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's quite common.

13
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:30,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, you know, walk from this point to that point.

14
00:01:30,784 --> 00:01:31,485
[SPEAKER_01]: How many steps?

15
00:01:31,605 --> 00:01:32,125
[SPEAKER_01]: 30 steps?

16
00:01:32,145 --> 00:01:34,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Same for anybody posture, when they're sitting.

17
00:01:34,247 --> 00:01:39,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, when the Buddha says he's upon the wrong path, means he's going down the wrong direction.

18
00:01:40,649 --> 00:01:43,391
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means in the most accurate manner.

19
00:01:44,172 --> 00:01:47,054
[SPEAKER_00]: So, going down the wrong path, it's basically attending to things wrongly.

20
00:01:47,074 --> 00:01:52,439
[SPEAKER_00]: That means going down the wrong direction of your... of your efforts.

21
00:01:52,459 --> 00:01:53,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even the ones who are making the efforts.

22
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:55,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the example you gave.

23
00:01:55,341 --> 00:02:07,352
[SPEAKER_00]: When... when you're doing walking meditation, mostly today people, again, they're after... you're after an experience of peace or some... some sort of... some sort of...

24
00:02:10,855 --> 00:02:27,039
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no factual... well, you're after experience of peace, or some sort of experience, or some novelty, or the other, but... not many people would use the meditation to reflect upon the nature of the thoughts.

25
00:02:27,859 --> 00:02:40,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, that's all this is to talk about, as anything that really matters, is to not entertain the unwholesome, and to cultivate the wholesome, whether you're sitting, walking, lying down, or standing.

26
00:02:40,940 --> 00:02:44,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're talking, whether you're alone or with people.

27
00:02:44,541 --> 00:03:00,544
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, unfortunately, today, you get this, like a mass group retreat centers and so on, and then you get taught, like, walking meditation, you observe the soles of your feet and you try to, like, be as present as possible and catch the moment and so on.

28
00:03:01,284 --> 00:03:09,046
[SPEAKER_00]: All that is nothing other than just a focusing practice on a particular aspect of your body, on a particular, basically, type of perception, of touch.

29
00:03:09,926 --> 00:03:10,267
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

30
00:03:11,007 --> 00:03:26,983
[SPEAKER_00]: And it can be so... it can... it can produce a release for people, because most of the... most of the... rest of the... rest of the time, they spend... they spend their... their day in an unmindful state, very busy, very engaged, and then just focusing on one particular thing and refusing to give attention to everything else.

31
00:03:27,623 --> 00:03:30,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Seems like a way to go, because it provides you with a degree of peace.

32
00:03:31,227 --> 00:03:34,029
[SPEAKER_00]: But that peace is only relative to your lifestyle beforehand.

33
00:03:34,790 --> 00:03:39,394
[SPEAKER_00]: That peace is not the measure of your understanding and wisdom that comes if you do the dhamma correctly.

34
00:03:40,235 --> 00:03:42,957
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not a hard thing to see that.

35
00:03:43,037 --> 00:04:00,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Just see when you're out of the retreat, how much of your composure you can keep when you go back to your daily life, to your other things, dealing with things, whether you'll still be able to remain impenetrable to the unwholesome states as you're walking, sitting, lying down or standing.

36
00:04:02,031 --> 00:04:02,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people won't.

37
00:04:02,971 --> 00:04:08,353
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they have to go back for retreats, prolonging retreats, doing longer retreats, more intense retreats, all that.

38
00:04:08,973 --> 00:04:17,416
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they say, if I focus on my feet, walking on the sand, then I don't think any sensual thoughts.

39
00:04:17,436 --> 00:04:19,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the thing, that's the thing.

40
00:04:21,637 --> 00:04:24,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Where you focus is not the root of the problem.

41
00:04:26,642 --> 00:04:32,245
[SPEAKER_00]: The root of the problem is not understanding the nature of unwholesome, and because of that, being subject to it.

42
00:04:32,965 --> 00:04:38,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't know how, even unwittingly, you entertain it, you fuel it, you feed it.

43
00:04:39,408 --> 00:04:40,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you end up acting out of it.

44
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:44,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, think of unwholesome thoughts as a problem.

45
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:48,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, proliferating unwholesome thoughts are a further problem.

46
00:04:49,287 --> 00:04:58,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what happens when people do the focusing practice, they would focus on one particular thing and refuse to give attention to everything else, including the problem, the problem of unwholesome.

47
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:02,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then they can feel like, yeah, this is really working.

48
00:05:02,290 --> 00:05:12,553
[SPEAKER_00]: And, I mean, I'm not saying there's absolutely, like, no benefit whatsoever, but I'm saying the benefit is only relative for those who are distracted most of the other time.

49
00:05:13,831 --> 00:05:19,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Distracted with duty, with family, with sensuality, or just with your views, in some cases.

50
00:05:20,075 --> 00:05:26,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is to recognize that, like, yeah, not giving attention to the problem can help you, but that's not solving the problem.

51
00:05:26,439 --> 00:05:28,861
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just managing it, dealing with it temporarily.

52
00:05:29,381 --> 00:05:30,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the point.

53
00:05:30,562 --> 00:05:36,666
[SPEAKER_00]: You can practice and further this type of focusing meditation only in such retreat settings.

54
00:05:37,206 --> 00:05:45,850
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't sustain that out in the world, or you can't sustain that if you have to climb up and down the mountain every single day for Pindapar for five kilometers in rain or whatever else.

55
00:05:46,350 --> 00:05:48,471
[SPEAKER_00]: You can try, but you're going to burn out very soon.

56
00:05:49,572 --> 00:06:01,877
[SPEAKER_00]: As you see many, many, many cases that, well, we both kind of witnessed, with the monks who would go in the great seclusion, focused on some sort of technique to do it, and then just after a week or two, you can't handle it anymore.

57
00:06:03,567 --> 00:06:04,228
[SPEAKER_00]: You need something.

58
00:06:04,548 --> 00:06:08,152
[SPEAKER_00]: You need some reference point, because, okay, now you stopped attending.

59
00:06:08,513 --> 00:06:10,314
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just focusing on one thing, but that's it.

60
00:06:10,595 --> 00:06:11,656
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a wall.

61
00:06:11,676 --> 00:06:12,277
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a dead end.

62
00:06:12,877 --> 00:06:21,766
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only benefit you were getting before, from your meditation practice, wasn't because of what you were focusing on, it's because of what you were not focusing on, which was the unwholesome.

63
00:06:22,567 --> 00:06:24,389
[SPEAKER_00]: You were not focusing on unwholesome.

64
00:06:24,449 --> 00:06:25,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the peace you were getting.

65
00:06:26,765 --> 00:06:37,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you arrived and not focusing on a wholesome, because you're focusing on something else, which sooner or later, environment will prevent you to do so, or you're just going to run out of the of the most obvious inspiration to do so.

66
00:06:38,049 --> 00:06:38,950
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what you're going to do?

67
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:47,453
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just back in the same unwholesome, which remain intact, because you haven't been understanding it, discerning it, resolving it.

68
00:06:47,673 --> 00:06:53,155
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been ignoring it, through watching the soles of your feet, through watching the tip of your nose, and so on.

69
00:06:54,265 --> 00:06:54,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

70
00:06:55,526 --> 00:07:00,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that people can experience peaceful feelings when they do focusing meditation.

71
00:07:00,688 --> 00:07:04,310
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point here is that won't result in uprooting the problem.

72
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:05,350
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only covering it up.

73
00:07:06,991 --> 00:07:11,834
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only approve the problem if you start looking at it for what it is, directly.

74
00:07:12,994 --> 00:07:16,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the part of the sutta you read just talks about.

75
00:07:17,755 --> 00:07:18,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you recognize.

76
00:07:18,956 --> 00:07:25,922
[SPEAKER_00]: The work is not about me doing walking meditation for eight hours a day, or focusing on my nostrils all the time.

77
00:07:25,942 --> 00:07:35,570
[SPEAKER_00]: The work is recognizing whether the mind is affected by ill-will, affected by greed, affected by delusion, affected by unwholesome states, and then not entertaining that.

78
00:07:36,191 --> 00:07:40,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter whether I'm walking, sitting, lying down, or standing.

79
00:07:40,614 --> 00:07:41,335
[SPEAKER_01]: What posture?

80
00:07:42,075 --> 00:07:47,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the posture is your reference point that you don't need to interfere with, if you know where the problem is.

81
00:07:47,879 --> 00:07:50,641
[SPEAKER_00]: And the problem is mind affected by unwholesome.

82
00:07:52,062 --> 00:07:57,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So, indeed, sometimes, when it's just too much to deal with, yeah, just ignore it.

83
00:07:58,066 --> 00:08:02,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't pay any attention to it until it calms down, so that you can go back to it and deal with it.

84
00:08:04,491 --> 00:08:04,611
[SPEAKER_00]: But

85
00:08:06,142 --> 00:08:19,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Adopting a practice of just constantly not looking at the problem and focusing on something else, as a way of release from the problem, it's not going to resolve anything, because there's absolutely no discernment there, no wisdom, no understanding, because you're not looking at it.

86
00:08:20,149 --> 00:08:20,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction.

87
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:22,410
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a form of distraction.

88
00:08:22,751 --> 00:08:26,412
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why... that's why the Buddha did say, when he gave that simile, he says,

89
00:08:29,418 --> 00:08:38,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being free... not being free from sensuality, these samanas and brāhmaṇas, they pre-meditate, mis-meditate, over-meditate, under-meditate, but they don't meditate.

90
00:08:39,401 --> 00:08:40,342
[SPEAKER_00]: And it gives a simile.

91
00:08:40,802 --> 00:08:48,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It says they are like... like an owl hunting for a mouse, or a cat waiting to pounce on a fish, or... or... or... or... similes.

92
00:08:48,665 --> 00:08:55,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, similes of preying upon something, of focusing on one moment where you act, where you catch it, where you grab it.

93
00:08:57,256 --> 00:08:59,779
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, that's meditating with sensuality.

94
00:09:00,199 --> 00:09:05,164
[SPEAKER_00]: That's looking completely down the wrong path.

95
00:09:05,324 --> 00:09:10,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's, again, just imagine yourself preying upon something.

96
00:09:10,329 --> 00:09:13,252
[SPEAKER_00]: You're waiting for, I don't know, a mouse to come out of this hole.

97
00:09:13,873 --> 00:09:17,036
[SPEAKER_00]: You mustn't skip a beat, because he might escape you.

98
00:09:18,232 --> 00:09:30,495
[SPEAKER_00]: And see whether that's different from when people try to not skip a beat when they're observing the soles of their feet, or not skip a beat when they're focusing on the nostrils, or the belly movement, or whatever.

99
00:09:31,155 --> 00:09:34,576
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up cultivating the same attitude of preying upon something.

100
00:09:34,716 --> 00:09:37,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Preying upon something that you clearly think has value.

101
00:09:38,597 --> 00:09:39,937
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to resolve all your problems.

102
00:09:39,997 --> 00:09:41,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Catching this mouse will fix all my problems.

103
00:09:42,038 --> 00:09:46,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Catching these perfect moments that come and go in front of me will purify my mind.

104
00:09:47,976 --> 00:09:48,856
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a completely...

105
00:09:48,876 --> 00:09:51,417
[SPEAKER_00]: He said people who meditate like that, meditate with sensuality.

106
00:09:52,018 --> 00:09:56,679
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they pre-meditate, mis-meditate, over-meditate, under-meditate and so on, but they don't meditate.

107
00:10:00,021 --> 00:10:13,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as you walk in, a thought of cruelty arises, and they don't tolerate it, which means... Yeah, which means you don't... like... thought of cruelty arises, means... there's your work then for you, in its entirety.

108
00:10:15,547 --> 00:10:16,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Thought of cruelty.

109
00:10:17,372 --> 00:10:17,993
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

110
00:10:18,033 --> 00:10:18,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

111
00:10:19,754 --> 00:10:22,156
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't... you don't stop everything you've been doing.

112
00:10:22,176 --> 00:10:26,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't... you... like... just give it all up, because I will never...

113
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:28,501
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want ever to have such thoughts again.

114
00:10:29,162 --> 00:10:31,424
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do that, because... well, it's not up to you.

115
00:10:31,584 --> 00:10:33,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Those thoughts will be presented themselves.

116
00:10:33,465 --> 00:10:42,073
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order for you to not be victim of those presentations of ill-will or sensuality, you need to understand how you engage with it.

117
00:10:42,837 --> 00:10:52,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't understand the habits of your engagement with those thoughts, while they're simultaneously present there, if your sole response to that is look away.

118
00:10:53,221 --> 00:10:54,182
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just look away.

119
00:10:54,422 --> 00:10:55,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Just ignore it.

120
00:10:55,122 --> 00:10:56,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... just concentrate on my breath.

121
00:10:56,523 --> 00:10:57,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Concentrate on my breath.

122
00:10:57,303 --> 00:10:57,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop thinking about it.

123
00:10:57,983 --> 00:10:58,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop thinking about it.

124
00:11:00,124 --> 00:11:09,348
[SPEAKER_00]: And on top of that, you're going to wind yourself up much more, because thinking that you must stop thinking, is another thought, that you're thinking, in order to not think.

125
00:11:11,125 --> 00:11:18,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it becomes so intense and sort of unpleasant.

126
00:11:18,835 --> 00:11:21,399
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize the thought of cruelty.

127
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,061
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, that's enough.

128
00:11:23,562 --> 00:11:24,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Very often.

129
00:11:24,203 --> 00:11:27,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, your recognition might not be clear enough.

130
00:11:28,149 --> 00:11:29,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you need to endure it.

131
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:44,817
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to recognize whereby... how much of the thought arises on its own, and then you have to recognize where the... where the line is, where if you cross, then it's you doing it, you entertaining it, you getting involved with it, you trying to get rid of it.

132
00:11:45,057 --> 00:11:47,438
[SPEAKER_00]: All of those are ways of fueling the thought.

133
00:11:49,421 --> 00:11:52,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Including you actively trying to ignore it.

134
00:11:52,802 --> 00:11:54,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would... like, there is a thought in your mind.

135
00:11:54,742 --> 00:11:56,403
[SPEAKER_00]: I must focus on my soles of my feet.

136
00:11:56,523 --> 00:11:56,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

137
00:11:57,423 --> 00:12:03,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're trying to avoid the arisen thought in your mind that you think you must not have.

138
00:12:03,965 --> 00:12:09,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And by trying to avoid things that are disagreeable to you, what are you doing?

139
00:12:10,427 --> 00:12:12,567
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing exactly the thing you shouldn't be doing.

140
00:12:12,707 --> 00:12:14,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting out of craving in regard to how you're feeling.

141
00:12:15,688 --> 00:12:16,848
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the unpleasant feeling.

142
00:12:17,029 --> 00:12:17,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Get rid of it.

143
00:12:17,629 --> 00:12:18,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Get rid of it.

144
00:12:18,209 --> 00:12:18,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Get rid of it.

145
00:12:20,037 --> 00:12:21,178
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the pleasant feeling.

146
00:12:21,558 --> 00:12:22,319
[SPEAKER_00]: I must have it longer.

147
00:12:22,399 --> 00:12:23,160
[SPEAKER_01]: I must have it longer.

148
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:26,823
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a recognition that this is a cruel thought.

149
00:12:27,683 --> 00:12:28,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

150
00:12:28,364 --> 00:12:29,325
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your doing.

151
00:12:30,085 --> 00:12:31,807
[SPEAKER_00]: The cruel thought presented itself.

152
00:12:32,027 --> 00:12:33,248
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a cruel thought.

153
00:12:33,268 --> 00:12:33,909
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mine.

154
00:12:34,009 --> 00:12:34,669
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a cruel thought.

155
00:12:34,709 --> 00:12:36,731
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like somebody comes right here in front of your door.

156
00:12:38,032 --> 00:12:39,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is a public space.

157
00:12:39,774 --> 00:12:40,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Your kuti is yours.

158
00:12:40,794 --> 00:12:41,495
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't come in.

159
00:12:41,975 --> 00:12:43,697
[SPEAKER_00]: But they have every right to stand in front of your kuti.

160
00:12:44,357 --> 00:12:47,120
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you feel affected by that, that's on you.

161
00:12:48,373 --> 00:12:55,200
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you come out of your kuti and start to chase them, well, now you actually went into something that's not your domain, not your ownership, and trying to control it.

162
00:12:56,681 --> 00:12:59,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then means you left kuti unattended, and then other things can come in.

163
00:13:00,866 --> 00:13:02,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're everywhere and nowhere.

164
00:13:03,828 --> 00:13:05,971
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same, in the same manner, the thing cannot come in.

165
00:13:07,112 --> 00:13:10,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever thought is, unless you invite it in, unless you drag it in.

166
00:13:11,289 --> 00:13:12,630
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what happens with sensuality.

167
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:22,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Either sensuality, as in like agreeable desire, or disagreeable, something you want to get rid of, it just comes in front of you.

168
00:13:22,355 --> 00:13:24,536
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's agreeable, you are the one who brings it in.

169
00:13:24,977 --> 00:13:27,598
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's disagreeable, you are the one who goes out and tries to chase it.

170
00:13:28,158 --> 00:13:30,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you overstep your boundary.

171
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:33,441
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you're subjected to it.

172
00:13:33,521 --> 00:13:34,662
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're affected by it.

173
00:13:39,029 --> 00:13:47,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's that another sutta where the Buddha did say, while he was still unenlightened bodhisattva, when he was in the... it's called fear and dread, nājā-mikāya.

174
00:13:48,055 --> 00:13:57,261
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said, he'd be staying in this frightening forest and groves with wild animals, and then he'd be doing walking meditation at night.

175
00:13:58,102 --> 00:14:03,065
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, if there is a... if he would hear some sound or something, or have a frightening thought,

176
00:14:05,248 --> 00:14:09,229
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't change what he was doing, which is walking with it.

177
00:14:09,249 --> 00:14:10,109
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't stop walking.

178
00:14:11,009 --> 00:14:11,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry.

179
00:14:11,669 --> 00:14:16,050
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't stop walking until he dispels that unwholesome state of mind.

180
00:14:17,230 --> 00:14:18,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he might change the posture.

181
00:14:19,430 --> 00:14:31,012
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same manner, whether he was seated, standing, sitting or lying down, he wouldn't change the posture before... unless he first removed the unwholesome state that had arisen.

182
00:14:31,893 --> 00:14:33,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And you remove the unwholesome state,

183
00:14:34,415 --> 00:14:35,416
[SPEAKER_00]: By not acting out of it.

184
00:14:36,496 --> 00:14:37,617
[SPEAKER_00]: By not trying to get rid of it.

185
00:14:37,757 --> 00:14:39,698
[SPEAKER_00]: By not trying to prolong it.

186
00:14:39,738 --> 00:14:40,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what it means.

187
00:14:40,999 --> 00:14:45,221
[SPEAKER_00]: I wouldn't change the posture until this state is dispelled.

188
00:14:46,422 --> 00:14:53,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if the unwholesome state came up, and now he stopped walking and done something else, means he acted out of it.

189
00:14:53,886 --> 00:14:56,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already a way of fueling it.

190
00:14:57,598 --> 00:15:11,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you acted out of it in a form of trying to prolong it, as in unwholesome state of sensuality, or trying to deny it, get rid of it as quickly as possible, as in unwholesome state of ill will, or fear, or anything disagreeable.

191
00:15:13,163 --> 00:15:21,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, you recognize, and then, how much of it arises on its own, and then, you don't either add to it, or take away from it.

192
00:15:22,906 --> 00:15:25,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it subsides, then you change the posture.

193
00:15:28,050 --> 00:15:29,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do that long enough?

194
00:15:30,011 --> 00:15:38,153
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do that long enough, any... no matter how subtle ways of fueling the unwholesome states of your mind you've been entertaining, they will all fade.

195
00:15:38,794 --> 00:15:41,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just... you're uprooting it left and right.

196
00:15:42,595 --> 00:15:43,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Bit by bit by bit.

197
00:15:44,335 --> 00:15:48,097
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can uproot things, but it takes time for them to sort of wither and die.

198
00:15:49,977 --> 00:15:55,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But one thing for sure, they'll never wither and die, and you certainly never uprooted them, if you keep looking elsewhere.

199
00:15:57,244 --> 00:16:10,134
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep focusing on your nostrils for the sake of avoiding the thought and not having to think about anything and not having to experience displeasure, if you keep focusing on the soles of your feet because you're trying to catch a perfect moment and develop this ultimate peace, they will never be disturbed.

200
00:16:13,317 --> 00:16:18,001
[SPEAKER_00]: The non-disturbance doesn't come from not experiencing disturbing things.

201
00:16:19,342 --> 00:16:24,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-disturbance comes from not being disturbed by the disturbing things.

202
00:16:26,043 --> 00:16:32,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as you're concerned with disturbing things, not with your response in regard to disturbing things, you're looking in the wrong direction.

203
00:16:32,587 --> 00:16:33,768
[SPEAKER_00]: You are down the wrong path.

204
00:16:48,667 --> 00:16:50,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Something to do, basically.

205
00:16:50,508 --> 00:16:57,712
[SPEAKER_00]: You're focused on doing things, staring at things, perceiving things, observing things, but not looking your attitude behind that.

206
00:16:58,532 --> 00:17:02,594
[SPEAKER_00]: The intentionality behind, the motivation behind, the craving behind.

207
00:17:03,214 --> 00:17:04,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving is always behind.

208
00:17:04,735 --> 00:17:05,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always in regard to.

209
00:17:06,797 --> 00:17:10,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you never look in that direction of that background, you're never going to see it.

210
00:17:11,001 --> 00:17:27,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, there's these ideas that, by being in a particular posture, cross-legged, lotus position, back straight, spine erect, loosened, loose belly, all these posturings,

211
00:17:29,382 --> 00:17:33,844
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't do the work for you, but that is a game that you play.

212
00:17:34,284 --> 00:17:35,544
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a little ritual.

213
00:17:35,744 --> 00:17:40,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Little motions you go through to create the significance of what you're about to do.

214
00:17:40,526 --> 00:17:49,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you just sit down, stare at the wall, or look down the hill, and contemplate, why do you suffer when you can't fulfill your desires?

215
00:17:49,889 --> 00:17:52,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Or why do you suffer when you experience something disagreeable?

216
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:55,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you suffer when you're paired with things you don't want?

217
00:17:55,671 --> 00:17:57,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you suffer when you're separated from things you want?

218
00:17:59,244 --> 00:18:01,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there's not much to it that you can perform.

219
00:18:02,786 --> 00:18:05,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's for many people, it's almost just abstract.

220
00:18:05,708 --> 00:18:15,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they need something more palpable, which is on a level of sort of mechanical performance, doing things with your body, particular posture, particular way of walking.

221
00:18:15,414 --> 00:18:18,096
[SPEAKER_00]: I must be stepping on the outsides of my soles or whatever.

222
00:18:18,116 --> 00:18:19,197
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't be walking like this.

223
00:18:19,237 --> 00:18:20,037
[SPEAKER_00]: I must walk slowly.

224
00:18:20,057 --> 00:18:20,898
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't walk fast.

225
00:18:22,007 --> 00:18:26,430
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is being concerned with a particular... with a particular content of your perception.

226
00:18:26,550 --> 00:18:28,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see the nature behind it.

227
00:18:28,311 --> 00:18:35,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, failing to recognize the enduring feeling in regard to it.

228
00:18:35,677 --> 00:18:39,479
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess, athletic meditation.

229
00:18:39,719 --> 00:18:41,221
[SPEAKER_01]: How long do you sit?

230
00:18:41,541 --> 00:18:42,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Two hours?

231
00:18:42,882 --> 00:18:44,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Some guys can sit for two weeks.

232
00:18:44,975 --> 00:18:51,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's all based, as I said, it all revolves around trying to perceive, trying to catch, preying upon something.

233
00:18:51,918 --> 00:18:54,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you would have to go through these specialized motions.

234
00:18:55,820 --> 00:18:58,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you walk too fast, you can't be catching something.

235
00:18:58,641 --> 00:19:01,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you must walk slowly, slow down, stand, stand, and just look at it.

236
00:19:02,862 --> 00:19:04,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, just, just grab it as it comes out.

237
00:19:05,283 --> 00:19:06,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognize it, and that's it.

238
00:19:08,958 --> 00:19:12,640
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's nothing else than meditating with that same sensuality that you're trying to overcome.

239
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:19,383
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there's no... no unwholesome thoughts arising, what do I do then?

240
00:19:20,563 --> 00:19:28,166
[SPEAKER_00]: If no unwholesome... well, then you're either an arahant, or you don't know what unwholesome thoughts are, so you think they're not arising while they're fully there.

241
00:19:29,547 --> 00:19:30,587
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't be the third option.

242
00:19:30,848 --> 00:19:32,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ask yourself, am I an arahant?

243
00:19:32,388 --> 00:19:32,548
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

244
00:19:33,009 --> 00:19:36,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there are unwholesome thoughts, which means I need to look harder.

245
00:19:37,210 --> 00:19:46,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Or change the criteria for what constitutes a wholesome thought, unwholesome thought for me.

246
00:19:46,235 --> 00:19:56,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Because having a peaceful experience... That doesn't mean that it's not unwholesome.

247
00:19:58,321 --> 00:20:02,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Peaceful experience is unwholesome, if you don't understand it.

248
00:20:08,098 --> 00:20:09,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, not ultimately unwholesome.

249
00:20:09,978 --> 00:20:13,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Not unwholesome on the level of raging sensuality or mad ill-will.

250
00:20:14,879 --> 00:20:20,020
[SPEAKER_00]: But from the point of view of ultimate, complete, thorough liberation, yeah.

251
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:24,021
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no full understanding, to that degree, it's unwholesome.

252
00:20:24,061 --> 00:20:24,641
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said.

253
00:20:24,661 --> 00:20:29,742
[SPEAKER_00]: The neutral feeling, the most sublime feeling, praised by the wise, it's unwholesome if it's not known.

254
00:20:31,603 --> 00:20:33,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's wholesome if it's known.

255
00:20:37,527 --> 00:20:41,449
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a constant knowledge.

256
00:20:42,989 --> 00:20:43,449
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

257
00:20:43,529 --> 00:20:48,971
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no constant knowledge, in a sense of all-knowing, all the time, in every direction.

258
00:20:49,651 --> 00:20:51,612
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

259
00:20:51,672 --> 00:20:53,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge in regard to the horizon.

260
00:20:53,472 --> 00:20:55,333
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we talk about knowledge of arising.

261
00:20:56,553 --> 00:21:00,774
[SPEAKER_00]: The constant knowledge of the arising.

262
00:21:01,275 --> 00:21:02,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

263
00:21:04,397 --> 00:21:11,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Constant knowledge in regard to arising, it's other way of saying wisdom, in regard to dhammas, in regard to things that manifest.

264
00:21:12,301 --> 00:21:12,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's it.

265
00:21:12,941 --> 00:21:13,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Wisdom stays.

266
00:21:14,662 --> 00:21:20,385
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why if you develop wisdom in regard to nature of things, that wisdom stays.

267
00:21:20,745 --> 00:21:25,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is always... things are always present in one way or the other, which means wisdom in regard to them, it's always present.

268
00:21:25,948 --> 00:21:26,648
[SPEAKER_00]: That's nibbāna.

269
00:21:27,829 --> 00:21:31,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Fully developed principle, like that, is what nibbāna is.

270
00:21:34,724 --> 00:21:35,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Regenerating wisdom.

271
00:21:36,465 --> 00:21:37,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's simultaneously arising.

272
00:21:37,885 --> 00:21:41,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever it's manifested, that is wisely understood.

273
00:21:42,027 --> 00:21:47,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Its nature has been fully abandoned, disregarded, in a sense of no appropriation, no assumption.

274
00:21:48,630 --> 00:21:56,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So... Because you cannot have things arising for you.

275
00:21:58,274 --> 00:22:02,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're just locked in some... buried in some cave without any perceptions.

276
00:22:03,445 --> 00:22:08,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, dhammas are right in front of you all the time, as you thought, as absence of sights, absence of sounds.

277
00:22:09,048 --> 00:22:12,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are real manifesting things, as such.

278
00:22:13,390 --> 00:22:20,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as the five aggregates are there functioning, there is the five aggregates of phenomena, the five aggregates of manifestation.

279
00:22:20,733 --> 00:22:25,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how much of a content within that you might have or not.

280
00:22:26,996 --> 00:22:32,639
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why trying to avoid sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, that would compare that to a blind and a deaf man,

281
00:22:33,351 --> 00:22:35,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to somebody who's developing wisdom.

282
00:22:36,772 --> 00:22:38,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you avoid the unwholesome.

283
00:22:39,212 --> 00:22:41,853
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't engage with the unwholesome sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

284
00:22:41,873 --> 00:22:43,654
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't entertain them.

285
00:22:43,734 --> 00:22:49,975
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's just replacing one very unwholesome content with not unwholesome content.

286
00:22:50,075 --> 00:22:54,516
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to be free from either, you need to fully understand the nature of that content.

287
00:22:55,537 --> 00:23:00,758
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't get there just by staring at the content and expecting content to tell you what its nature is.

288
00:23:02,397 --> 00:23:08,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that would mean people could almost accidentally stumble upon enlightenment, just by staring and suddenly they get it.

289
00:23:08,559 --> 00:23:09,059
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't.

290
00:23:09,279 --> 00:23:13,700
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to develop it.

291
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:29,204
[SPEAKER_01]: In another sutra, in Anguttara Nikaya, the Buddha's talking to Anuruddha, one of his disciples, he says, this dhamma is for one with few desires.

292
00:23:30,430 --> 00:23:32,091
[SPEAKER_01]: But for one with strong desires.

293
00:23:34,173 --> 00:23:44,662
[SPEAKER_01]: With reference to what was this said, it says, when a bhikkhu is one with few desires, he does not desire, let people know me, to be one with few desires.

294
00:23:46,123 --> 00:23:50,364
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is content, he does not desire that people know me to be one who is content.

295
00:23:50,924 --> 00:23:55,985
[SPEAKER_01]: When he resorts to solitude, he does not desire that people know me to be one who resorts to solitude.

296
00:23:56,845 --> 00:24:01,506
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is energetic, he does not desire that people know me to be energetic.

297
00:24:02,206 --> 00:24:05,247
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is mindful, he does not want people to know that he is mindful.

298
00:24:06,147 --> 00:24:09,168
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is composed, he does not want people to know that he is composed.

299
00:24:10,228 --> 00:24:13,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he is wise, he does not want people to know that he is wise.

300
00:24:14,935 --> 00:24:24,343
[SPEAKER_01]: When he delights in non-proliferation, he does not desire that people know me to be one who delights in non-proliferation.

301
00:24:25,303 --> 00:24:30,668
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this dhamma is for one with few desires, but with strong desires.

302
00:24:32,169 --> 00:24:33,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not seeking fame.

303
00:24:34,391 --> 00:24:35,111
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, yes.

304
00:24:35,592 --> 00:24:39,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, it's more... it's more fundamental than just the fame.

305
00:24:40,456 --> 00:24:41,917
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes down to...

306
00:24:43,682 --> 00:24:46,565
[SPEAKER_00]: See, all of the things that Buddha listed are wholesome things.

307
00:24:48,446 --> 00:24:54,171
[SPEAKER_00]: A few desires, solitude, mindfulness, wisdom, restraint.

308
00:24:55,472 --> 00:24:57,033
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's almost like a...

309
00:24:58,614 --> 00:25:00,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire can be also reflexive.

310
00:25:00,596 --> 00:25:03,779
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't necessarily need to be focused on the central content.

311
00:25:05,223 --> 00:25:14,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can be practicing renunciation in the forest and stuff, but then, on the reflexive manner, you can be... well, you can be developing a tribe, a country, a conceit.

312
00:25:14,966 --> 00:25:16,126
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be indulging that.

313
00:25:17,827 --> 00:25:28,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you really want your practice to make a difference to yourself, you need to practice on both of those levels, so to speak.

314
00:25:28,590 --> 00:25:31,671
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you need to be mindful of the motivation behind your practice.

315
00:25:35,648 --> 00:25:49,620
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if... say you want to go in the remote forest, but the motivation behind it is because the word will spread around that you go out in the remote forests, so you'll be, yeah, famous or simply conceited.

316
00:25:49,640 --> 00:25:52,382
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll experience pleasure on account of other knowing that.

317
00:25:53,523 --> 00:25:56,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that doesn't mean you shouldn't go to the forest, because going to the forest is still wholesome.

318
00:25:58,267 --> 00:26:03,972
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you go to a forest, if you go to a forest, you make the effort to re-root.

319
00:26:05,580 --> 00:26:18,969
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, the Buddha encouraged, when it comes down to eating, we can't just stop eating.

320
00:26:20,931 --> 00:26:27,275
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you eat with right motivation, then it's not unwholesome.

321
00:26:27,395 --> 00:26:30,818
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't eat with right motivation, then it's automatically unwholesome.

322
00:26:32,325 --> 00:26:37,646
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean, if you cannot eat with right motivation, you must stop eating.

323
00:26:37,666 --> 00:26:37,806
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

324
00:26:37,866 --> 00:26:42,267
[SPEAKER_00]: You just must work harder to be able to re-root the reasons for your eating.

325
00:26:42,807 --> 00:26:45,668
[SPEAKER_00]: To root them in wholesome, not root them in unwholesome.

326
00:26:48,268 --> 00:26:54,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you recognize, firstly, what is it that you're about to do, and then, secondly, why.

327
00:26:56,350 --> 00:26:59,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I want to go and keep all the dhūtanghas and live in the forest.

328
00:27:00,820 --> 00:27:01,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Very good.

329
00:27:01,361 --> 00:27:01,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

330
00:27:01,982 --> 00:27:02,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

331
00:27:02,803 --> 00:27:07,829
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would sometimes say, let those who want to go and live on the root of truth, live on the root of truth.

332
00:27:07,849 --> 00:27:11,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Let those who want to eat once a day, eat once a day or eat out of their bowl.

333
00:27:11,394 --> 00:27:12,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Not everybody must do it.

334
00:27:13,316 --> 00:27:17,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Because some people won't be able to help it, but do it out of conceit.

335
00:27:18,471 --> 00:27:19,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of pride.

336
00:27:19,972 --> 00:27:23,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of, I can sit motionless for 10 hours and I eat only once every three days.

337
00:27:24,175 --> 00:27:27,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Like giants, they started out doing each other in their practices.

338
00:27:28,458 --> 00:27:29,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Who's going to go further?

339
00:27:30,219 --> 00:27:31,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Harder asceticism.

340
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:36,103
[SPEAKER_00]: But, no second layer of that reflection I'm talking about.

341
00:27:36,683 --> 00:27:40,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just focus on doing these things that are deemed right.

342
00:27:40,065 --> 00:27:40,846
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, why?

343
00:27:42,427 --> 00:27:46,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they bringing the unwholesome to decrease and increasing wholesome?

344
00:27:46,550 --> 00:27:46,750
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

345
00:27:47,655 --> 00:27:53,678
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just winding you up stronger and stronger in your desire for fame or recognition or whatever.

346
00:27:55,959 --> 00:27:58,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, asceticism is almost always good.

347
00:28:00,381 --> 00:28:06,043
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you really want to make it beneficial, you have to then look at another step back and see why you're doing it.

348
00:28:06,703 --> 00:28:12,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, not necessarily dismiss it, if you find that it's done for wrong reasons, but change the reasons.

349
00:28:13,339 --> 00:28:18,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't change the reasons for unwholesome action, like sensuality, for example, or anger.

350
00:28:19,481 --> 00:28:21,562
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't say, oh, I'll reroute that in good.

351
00:28:21,642 --> 00:28:22,002
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

352
00:28:22,482 --> 00:28:26,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Because those things are cemented in unwholesome motivation, always.

353
00:28:27,464 --> 00:28:32,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Motivation of ignorance, lust, aversion, which means you must just abandon it.

354
00:28:33,226 --> 00:28:40,708
[SPEAKER_00]: But there are many things, such as eating, or even practicing, going to the forest, and so on, that you can make unwholesome.

355
00:28:42,111 --> 00:28:51,361
[SPEAKER_00]: There can be some people who live in a monastery, and because they're surrounded by others,

356
00:28:59,411 --> 00:29:05,978
[SPEAKER_01]: They become quite ascetic, quite, you know, many practices ascetic by sitting for many hours each and every day.

357
00:29:06,318 --> 00:29:10,923
[SPEAKER_01]: But as soon as they go and live by themselves, all those practices are gone.

358
00:29:11,263 --> 00:29:15,267
[SPEAKER_00]: It means they haven't been taking responsibility from the practice, for the practice, from the start.

359
00:29:15,647 --> 00:29:19,311
[SPEAKER_00]: They've been relying on the temple environment, other monks around them.

360
00:29:19,972 --> 00:29:21,133
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what kept them in check.

361
00:29:22,687 --> 00:29:28,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, once you're out of that setting, you just... well, you dissolve.

362
00:29:28,748 --> 00:29:45,931
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, even if it's not explicitly for their acknowledgement, you do it... you do it basically not with your own... taking on responsibility on yourself, but you do it because the environment, everybody does it, and if you don't do it, you stand out too much.

363
00:29:49,092 --> 00:29:50,752
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily a bad way to start.

364
00:29:51,561 --> 00:29:53,762
[SPEAKER_00]: If you recognize it, that's only a starting point.

365
00:29:54,722 --> 00:30:02,844
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, adhering to the environment and never changing it, for the sake of the value of the environment in itself, means, how do you know that?

366
00:30:05,225 --> 00:30:06,945
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you know where your motivation is rooted?

367
00:30:07,946 --> 00:30:12,687
[SPEAKER_00]: And on top of it, it can't possibly be good for you being surrounded by the people, even if it's other monks.

368
00:30:14,227 --> 00:30:18,049
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said... well, actually, it's very, very close to what we're talking about.

369
00:30:18,069 --> 00:30:18,590
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

370
00:30:19,090 --> 00:30:22,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Going to a solitude is always wholesome in itself.

371
00:30:22,492 --> 00:30:27,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yes, you can make it unwholesome, but you can undo that bit and still go to solitude.

372
00:30:28,616 --> 00:30:42,544
[SPEAKER_00]: There's that sutta where the Buddha said if a monk lives in the forest and he's lazy, sleeps all the time, thinks about sensuality, but he still stays in the forest,

373
00:30:43,459 --> 00:30:49,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, sooner or later, the mind will calm down, get used to the perceptions of the forest, and he'll abandon those unwholesome states.

374
00:30:51,444 --> 00:30:53,385
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is the benefit of the environment.

375
00:30:54,386 --> 00:31:03,772
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, counterpart to that monk is the monk who lives in a temple, frequented by lay people, not far from the town or the villages, surrounded by other monks.

376
00:31:03,812 --> 00:31:04,472
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not alone.

377
00:31:04,512 --> 00:31:04,933
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives there.

378
00:31:05,693 --> 00:31:12,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he's not spending his time thinking about sensuality, even if he's not lazy, even if he's not negligent, he will become so.

379
00:31:13,662 --> 00:31:17,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the environment of the crowd and other people, that's what's going to result into.

380
00:31:18,324 --> 00:31:18,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

381
00:31:19,025 --> 00:31:29,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, you can benefit from it initially, but not as a fixed in stone sort of principle that you must always be with 30 other monks.

382
00:31:31,652 --> 00:31:35,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you must always be like within the stone's throw away from the town or the village.

383
00:31:36,714 --> 00:31:37,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the contrary.

384
00:31:37,435 --> 00:31:42,517
[SPEAKER_00]: You read the suttas, you know, empty huts, empty forests, empty roots of the trees.

385
00:31:42,537 --> 00:31:43,458
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you waiting for?

386
00:31:44,398 --> 00:31:45,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can... people can do that.

387
00:31:45,779 --> 00:31:46,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't want to deal with any of this.

388
00:31:46,799 --> 00:31:47,740
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll run into the forest.

389
00:31:47,880 --> 00:31:48,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

390
00:31:48,741 --> 00:31:50,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It's done for unwholesome reasons.

391
00:31:51,662 --> 00:31:57,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But if they stay in that forest, they will have to correct those reasons or they will leave the forest and this road.

392
00:32:00,007 --> 00:32:08,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, the environment does... well, does direct... direct basically... direct you in your practice.

393
00:32:09,693 --> 00:32:16,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no matter of your restraint and diligence and everything else, if you live surrounded by others constantly, you cannot progress.

394
00:32:17,936 --> 00:32:26,218
[SPEAKER_01]: This is another point that says that this dhamma is for one who resorts to solitude.

395
00:32:26,839 --> 00:32:27,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

396
00:32:27,159 --> 00:32:29,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for one who delights in company.

397
00:32:30,100 --> 00:32:32,541
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, resorting to solitude means exactly that.

398
00:32:32,681 --> 00:32:36,542
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual physical solitude doesn't mean going to your bedroom and closing the door.

399
00:32:38,216 --> 00:32:41,579
[SPEAKER_00]: This goes on further.

400
00:32:44,681 --> 00:33:04,457
[SPEAKER_01]: When a bhikkhu resorts to solitude, bhikkhus, bhikkhunis, male lay followers, female lay followers, kings, royal ministers, and so on, approaching, in each case, with a mind that slants, slopes and climbs to seclusion, withdrawn,

401
00:33:06,142 --> 00:33:19,244
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not encouraging or entertaining them to stay any longer, because you don't want them there.

402
00:33:21,285 --> 00:33:22,165
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want them there.

403
00:33:22,185 --> 00:33:24,905
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you go even further, like you often end up hiding.

404
00:33:26,185 --> 00:33:33,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like literally, sort of, running under the rock, but if you see that people might see you, you avoid being seen.

405
00:33:35,509 --> 00:33:36,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

406
00:33:36,449 --> 00:33:39,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a deer, yeah.

407
00:33:39,551 --> 00:33:40,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean.

408
00:33:40,291 --> 00:33:47,715
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like... You know, there's that sutra where the Buddha reaffirmed Ananda because they were sewing the robes with a katina.

409
00:33:49,696 --> 00:33:56,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was just, you know, a monastery full of monks and many of them were probably sotāpānas, sakadāgāmīs, you know, which means relatively accomplished.

410
00:33:58,534 --> 00:34:07,897
[SPEAKER_00]: But what they were doing, they were socializing on account of a, you know, noble cause of sewing the robes, you know, for the auspicious gathering of the kathina.

411
00:34:09,017 --> 00:34:12,899
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the Buddha came in, he was like, what is this noise?

412
00:34:12,939 --> 00:34:15,639
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it was a band of fishermen in here.

413
00:34:16,580 --> 00:34:20,781
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, oh Lord, we're getting ready, we're sewing the robes, and then he just blasted it.

414
00:34:21,982 --> 00:34:23,263
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you still have work to do.

415
00:34:23,443 --> 00:34:28,308
[SPEAKER_00]: You should stop wasting your time with these distractions and basically go to solitude and practice.

416
00:34:29,949 --> 00:34:30,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

417
00:34:30,950 --> 00:34:36,555
[SPEAKER_00]: You can sort of say that, oh, yeah, you went to a forest or somebody went to a forest for conceited reasons.

418
00:34:37,395 --> 00:34:38,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's fine.

419
00:34:38,196 --> 00:34:41,018
[SPEAKER_00]: But going to the forest is never a bad thing.

420
00:34:43,280 --> 00:34:46,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And sooner or later, their reasons will become clear to them, which is

421
00:34:47,087 --> 00:34:50,109
[SPEAKER_00]: The point when they would either leave the forest, or leave those reasons.

422
00:34:50,930 --> 00:34:51,511
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see that.

423
00:34:51,531 --> 00:34:52,391
[SPEAKER_00]: You see that first-hand.

424
00:34:52,852 --> 00:35:05,101
[SPEAKER_00]: When new people come in a day, and they leave the teacher right away, because they want to go, they're just looking for... they know what they're doing, and then they spend first two years going from one place to the other place, to the other place, to the other place.

425
00:35:06,022 --> 00:35:08,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Feels like they tried it all, and then they disrobe.

426
00:35:08,763 --> 00:35:12,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they go straight into solitude, and then don't really know what they're doing.

427
00:35:12,585 --> 00:35:14,786
[SPEAKER_00]: They went for the wrong reasons, and then that becomes apparent.

428
00:35:15,386 --> 00:35:20,248
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, if they were to stay in that solitude, well, they might go mad, sure.

429
00:35:20,909 --> 00:35:27,852
[SPEAKER_00]: But if they don't go mad, and if they don't disrobe, those reasons will... they will... they'll be forced to abandon those unwholesome reasons.

430
00:35:28,818 --> 00:35:30,198
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha meant by that sutta.

431
00:35:30,258 --> 00:35:37,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he spends his time fantasizing about sensuality, sleeping all day long in the forest, the mind will adjust.

432
00:35:38,120 --> 00:35:44,062
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll stop doing those things, because forest is directly counterproductive for those things.

433
00:35:44,082 --> 00:35:50,363
[SPEAKER_00]: He refuses to leave the solitude, refuses to disrespect the solitude, basically, in terms of his views.

434
00:35:51,623 --> 00:35:53,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything that contradicts it will be abandoned.

435
00:35:54,972 --> 00:36:07,479
[SPEAKER_00]: The other monk is the one who is not constantly thinking about spirituality, who might be quite inspiring and restrained and everything else, but he never leaves his temple, he never leaves his monastery, he never leaves the proximity of people.

436
00:36:08,139 --> 00:36:09,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say people, I mean lay people and monks.

437
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:15,883
[SPEAKER_01]: When he talks to other followers, he encourages them... he gives talk which encourages them to stay.

438
00:36:16,584 --> 00:36:17,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

439
00:36:17,384 --> 00:36:21,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, okay, that's... that's the political side of running the monasteries.

440
00:36:21,666 --> 00:36:22,627
[SPEAKER_00]: We're not going to go into that.

441
00:36:24,267 --> 00:36:35,714
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, I'm just saying from the practice point of view, even if you're making loads of effort towards your restraint and everything, you do have to recognize what environment you're in.

442
00:36:35,814 --> 00:36:39,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Is most of the day that you spend, is it with other people, including other monks?

443
00:36:40,658 --> 00:36:48,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't talk to anyone, are there other monks and lay people around you, I don't know, eight hours per day of your waking time?

444
00:36:50,432 --> 00:36:52,353
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it is so, it means you're not in solitude.

445
00:36:52,834 --> 00:37:03,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you have a... you have a break in your room or something, but that's the... like a minority of your time spent in solitude, while the vast majority is just surrounded in the crowd.

446
00:37:03,181 --> 00:37:04,803
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the solitude in everybody.

447
00:37:04,863 --> 00:37:05,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the solitude.

448
00:37:05,883 --> 00:37:06,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly, yeah.

449
00:37:06,864 --> 00:37:10,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, people might call that sutta, well, the true solitude is the one without the greed, aversion, delusion.

450
00:37:10,627 --> 00:37:15,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and you'll arrive at that when you do recognize the validity of the physical solitude.

451
00:37:15,470 --> 00:37:16,711
[SPEAKER_00]: When you do that...

452
00:37:16,912 --> 00:37:17,192
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean...

453
00:37:18,040 --> 00:37:23,423
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's really, like, not that important, 90% of the suttas would not be talking about it.

454
00:37:27,045 --> 00:37:36,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, as I said, probably quite a noble saṅgha at the time, of Aryans and so on, were together sewing the rope.

455
00:37:38,011 --> 00:37:42,174
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha reprimanded them for socializing in such ridiculous, pointless manner.

456
00:37:43,045 --> 00:37:49,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone how much more today you would have that, when it's... most of the time, it's not even for such a lofty cause of solving the road.

457
00:37:50,662 --> 00:37:59,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like just, ah, well, we're washing spittoons together, and now we're sweeping the leaves together, and now we're doing this together, and now we're together, and we're together.

458
00:37:59,624 --> 00:38:00,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, we go and meditate together.

459
00:38:01,405 --> 00:38:03,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, we go and meditate together with lay people together.

460
00:38:03,965 --> 00:38:08,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, we have group retreats where you can't stay in the forest alone, but you must come here, and thirty of us will sit together.

461
00:38:08,846 --> 00:38:09,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, we eat together.

462
00:38:10,027 --> 00:38:11,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, we eat breakfast together.

463
00:38:11,547 --> 00:38:12,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, we do chores together.

464
00:38:13,027 --> 00:38:16,288
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, okay, so how many hours per day you spend together?

465
00:38:16,748 --> 00:38:18,308
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, those hours that you don't

466
00:38:20,809 --> 00:38:30,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, or in your bedroom, in the shower, toilet, work, or your bedroom, which is a paper wall next to another bedroom, with another person in it.

467
00:38:30,991 --> 00:38:32,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're never really alone.

468
00:38:34,271 --> 00:38:34,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean.

469
00:38:35,292 --> 00:38:39,792
[SPEAKER_00]: From that point of view, you will only be emulating the ideas of solitude and so on.

470
00:38:40,432 --> 00:38:42,193
[SPEAKER_00]: You're playing in the sand, pretty much.

471
00:38:44,033 --> 00:38:47,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Hold on.

472
00:38:48,034 --> 00:38:49,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to reiterate,

473
00:38:51,213 --> 00:39:02,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if somebody is in the solitude, avoiding socializing for unwholesome reasons, give them a year or two, those reasons will either have to change or they will leave.

474
00:39:03,763 --> 00:39:09,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the solitude, the eh... not socializing, well, will make it clear for them.

475
00:39:11,369 --> 00:39:12,230
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have it both.

476
00:39:12,770 --> 00:39:15,793
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have those wrong reasons for solitude and stay in the solitude.

477
00:39:19,147 --> 00:39:26,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality, laziness, non-diligence, not wanting to deal with people, avoiding, running away, basically.

478
00:39:27,032 --> 00:39:41,781
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all going to be highlighted, and then you're either going to give in to it, and abandon the solitude, or the solitude will straighten all of those knots out for you.

479
00:39:41,801 --> 00:39:44,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, because this inscription of solitude here,

480
00:39:45,739 --> 00:39:47,740
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't just mean freedom from greed.

481
00:39:47,760 --> 00:39:50,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It means the actual physical solitude.

482
00:39:51,362 --> 00:39:52,042
Yeah.

483
00:39:53,643 --> 00:39:58,405
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this dhamma is for one who is content, not for one who is discontent.

484
00:39:59,986 --> 00:40:10,591
[SPEAKER_01]: With reference to what was this said, here a bhikkhu is content with any kind of robes, alms food, lodgings and medicines and provisions for the sick.

485
00:40:10,791 --> 00:40:11,472
[SPEAKER_01]: He's content.

486
00:40:19,052 --> 00:40:23,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Because not being content, you could use that as a major distraction.

487
00:40:23,994 --> 00:40:24,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

488
00:40:24,434 --> 00:40:26,194
[SPEAKER_01]: For making robes.

489
00:40:27,715 --> 00:40:35,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, for making robes, for entertaining the crowds for the sake of support, because you need support for the monastery.

490
00:40:36,758 --> 00:40:42,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, as if none of those things are related to your choices and actions you made.

491
00:40:44,631 --> 00:40:50,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even trying to get more requisites than you need involves non-solitude.

492
00:40:51,197 --> 00:40:52,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, absolutely, yeah.

493
00:40:52,218 --> 00:41:00,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, even like, for example, again, I'm not talking about the temples, the cultural places where people gather and do the rituals.

494
00:41:00,785 --> 00:41:06,490
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm talking about, say, a monastery, which is, well, at least on paper, where monks go to train and practice.

495
00:41:07,671 --> 00:41:09,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that is like...

496
00:41:11,170 --> 00:41:22,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't, or you can't afford to be building it for the sake of some sort of ideal of having a great monastery, where it's going to be like this, like that.

497
00:41:22,243 --> 00:41:25,127
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only be built in response to the practical need.

498
00:41:26,729 --> 00:41:29,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything else is too much.

499
00:41:30,258 --> 00:41:41,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means everything else would then result in you having to maintain certain level of engagement with lay people because you need a certain threshold of funds to maintain the big place that you built.

500
00:41:42,544 --> 00:41:43,405
[SPEAKER_00]: That was unnecessary.

501
00:41:44,105 --> 00:41:49,588
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, that means that everything else becomes secondary to that.

502
00:41:51,069 --> 00:41:53,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Including, very often, the monk's solitude and practice.

503
00:41:54,651 --> 00:41:59,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh no, we all have to come and eat together because our big lay supporters, they like to see that.

504
00:42:02,205 --> 00:42:08,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they don't like what they see, as in, they don't see any monks, they all went to their kutis, they might stop coming.

505
00:42:09,031 --> 00:42:10,872
[SPEAKER_00]: If they stop coming, they'll stop making donations.

506
00:42:10,972 --> 00:42:15,036
[SPEAKER_00]: If they stop making donations, well, we can't maintain all these twenty kutis that we built unnecessarily.

507
00:42:16,077 --> 00:42:16,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

508
00:42:16,777 --> 00:42:19,400
[SPEAKER_00]: It's built in response to a practical need.

509
00:42:20,838 --> 00:42:34,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, hopefully, with the idea of zero maintenance in the future, you don't want to rely on having to have twenty monks all the time, so you can divide all the duties and chores, so that everybody can do everything, so things can stay functional.

510
00:42:35,869 --> 00:42:43,916
[SPEAKER_00]: You build simply with minimal maintenance, and then you factor in that tomorrow you might be alone.

511
00:42:45,577 --> 00:42:48,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if now there are ten monks, tomorrow you might be alone.

512
00:42:48,199 --> 00:42:49,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Will you be able to maintain all this?

513
00:42:50,991 --> 00:42:58,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can't, you shouldn't be going forward, if you're looking to how, sort of, establish a place for proper practice.

514
00:42:59,073 --> 00:43:12,295
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're looking how to establish institution, which is already a temple, even if you call it a forest monastery, well, that's a different... that's a different ballgame, but that's... that's not something that a forest monk or somebody who wants to practice the dhamma should be doing.

515
00:43:14,495 --> 00:43:16,776
[SPEAKER_01]: We're also being able to

516
00:43:19,732 --> 00:43:26,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Get, you know, get robes, alms food, medicines, shelters, to sort those things out.

517
00:43:26,053 --> 00:43:26,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's it.

518
00:43:26,634 --> 00:43:27,634
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all needed.

519
00:43:28,594 --> 00:43:32,795
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something we all agree in the Upasampada procedure.

520
00:43:33,515 --> 00:43:35,656
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the basis that I agree to.

521
00:43:36,276 --> 00:43:38,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything over that is extra.

522
00:43:39,396 --> 00:43:42,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything else over that is unnecessary, in a way.

523
00:43:43,517 --> 00:43:45,578
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I just want a different kind of...

524
00:43:47,087 --> 00:43:49,509
[SPEAKER_01]: But being able to sort... Are you recording, by the way?

525
00:43:49,529 --> 00:43:49,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

526
00:43:50,609 --> 00:43:51,450
[SPEAKER_00]: The time's going down.

527
00:43:51,470 --> 00:43:52,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

528
00:43:52,911 --> 00:43:59,296
[SPEAKER_01]: To be able to get those things and be content with the simple things, you know, being able to...

529
00:44:00,295 --> 00:44:01,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Organize shelter for yourself.

530
00:44:02,496 --> 00:44:03,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Know how to get alms.

531
00:44:03,976 --> 00:44:04,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Know how to fast.

532
00:44:05,716 --> 00:44:08,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Know how to deal with the body when it's sick.

533
00:44:08,257 --> 00:44:09,297
[SPEAKER_01]: What medicines to use.

534
00:44:09,737 --> 00:44:10,898
[SPEAKER_01]: How to sew your clothing.

535
00:44:11,098 --> 00:44:12,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, sure, sure, sure.

536
00:44:12,558 --> 00:44:14,859
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need that many people.

537
00:44:15,099 --> 00:44:17,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what nisaya should be all about.

538
00:44:17,999 --> 00:44:18,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a teacher.

539
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:20,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, you teach them in dhamma.

540
00:44:22,803 --> 00:44:27,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on top of that, you literally equip them so that they can live alone.

541
00:44:28,085 --> 00:44:29,225
[SPEAKER_00]: They can live independently.

542
00:44:29,706 --> 00:44:34,167
[SPEAKER_00]: They'll know how to look after themselves in that basic survival manner.

543
00:44:34,968 --> 00:44:43,331
[SPEAKER_00]: If you live in the forest, you cut, you fall, you smash your elbow, twist your ankle, or have basic recognition like, oh, hold on, am I sick?

544
00:44:43,411 --> 00:44:43,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I this?

545
00:44:43,931 --> 00:44:44,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I that?

546
00:44:44,531 --> 00:44:46,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Just knowing certain practical things how to survive.

547
00:44:48,640 --> 00:44:51,482
[SPEAKER_00]: If you prepare that with dumb understanding, then yeah, you can go and live anywhere.

548
00:44:51,502 --> 00:44:55,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that gives you, you know, being able to survive by yourself.

549
00:44:55,286 --> 00:44:56,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

550
00:44:56,427 --> 00:44:59,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it gives you the confidence, contentment.

551
00:44:59,229 --> 00:45:00,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, sure, sure.

552
00:45:01,531 --> 00:45:02,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, content, and that's all.

553
00:45:02,572 --> 00:45:04,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And another thing is...

554
00:45:05,419 --> 00:45:11,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimately, the dhamma is that which makes you content, even if you don't have the basic requisites that you need.

555
00:45:12,205 --> 00:45:13,727
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't threaten your existence.

556
00:45:14,307 --> 00:45:19,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You get sick, prospect of severe illness, maybe death, you don't go distraught.

557
00:45:19,893 --> 00:45:22,395
[SPEAKER_00]: If you've been practicing dhamma, you won't be able to be distraught.

558
00:45:23,456 --> 00:45:27,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, you'll seek out the medicine, but not at all cost.

559
00:45:29,906 --> 00:45:35,669
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see that there were cases of monks who spent 20-30 years in the forest, in the jungle, and then they got sick.

560
00:45:35,749 --> 00:45:41,212
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, they needed to stay in the hospital for the sake of, basically, staying alive.

561
00:45:41,272 --> 00:45:44,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And even that, that's not something they wanted to do.

562
00:45:44,494 --> 00:45:45,634
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't want to stay in hospital.

563
00:45:47,375 --> 00:45:52,218
[SPEAKER_00]: The bare minimum, just to be repaired, and they went back into the forest.

564
00:45:52,258 --> 00:45:54,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what you value, that's what you got used to.

565
00:45:56,717 --> 00:45:59,658
[SPEAKER_00]: But many people today act out of life is the greatest gain.

566
00:46:00,818 --> 00:46:05,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else is worth doing if it sort of even exposes a bit of a bit of risk to it.

567
00:46:06,100 --> 00:46:07,881
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can live your lay life like that.

568
00:46:07,901 --> 00:46:08,441
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

569
00:46:08,501 --> 00:46:12,462
[SPEAKER_00]: But as a monk, that's kind of completely counterproductive.

570
00:46:12,502 --> 00:46:14,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone spending your time worrying about your future.

571
00:46:16,700 --> 00:46:18,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Building a monk's hospital and what...

572
00:46:18,601 --> 00:46:27,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's nice if lay people do it for whatever purpose, but if you're doing it out of your own existential concern, oh, what am I going to do when I get old, if I get sick?

573
00:46:28,245 --> 00:46:34,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you should have thought about it when you haven't been practicing, because arahant cannot possibly worry about those things.

574
00:46:35,735 --> 00:46:38,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, even anāgāmī, for goodness sake, can't worry about those things.

575
00:46:38,516 --> 00:46:41,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Even sotāpanna can't worry about those things to the same extent.

576
00:46:43,278 --> 00:46:51,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But even for people who are, for example, live outdoors, and have, you know, survival skills, even they, to an extent, do not worry about old age.

577
00:46:52,582 --> 00:46:52,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because...

578
00:46:53,994 --> 00:46:57,096
[SPEAKER_01]: They've been surviving by themselves their whole lives.

579
00:46:57,136 --> 00:46:59,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Many examples of people living out in the woods.

580
00:46:59,698 --> 00:47:05,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, not necessarily, yeah, exactly, not necessarily monks or religious people, just in general.

581
00:47:06,323 --> 00:47:09,365
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, you see, it's quite, it's quite a feat.

582
00:47:10,086 --> 00:47:11,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's quite inspiring to see.

583
00:47:13,048 --> 00:47:22,335
[SPEAKER_00]: At that level of independence, even in a, like, completely, again, not following Buddha's teaching or whatever, but just live simply, remotely, roughly,

584
00:47:23,548 --> 00:47:24,449
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's perfectly fine.

585
00:47:25,229 --> 00:47:26,690
[SPEAKER_00]: And even when he isn't fine, he's fine.

586
00:47:28,791 --> 00:47:37,575
[SPEAKER_00]: He's still not... not the... not the... not the reason, not the cause for giving it all up and running back into the crowd where people look after you.

587
00:47:37,776 --> 00:47:41,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, it's... the life is not going to last forever.

588
00:47:43,519 --> 00:47:46,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you live a hundred years, it's not long enough.

589
00:47:47,021 --> 00:47:51,303
[SPEAKER_00]: If your mind is bent on sensuality, if your mind is bent on comfort and avoiding pain,

590
00:47:54,204 --> 00:48:04,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is better to live a shorter life with a greater quality to it, quality in terms of practicing the dhamma and dedication towards that, even if sometimes compromises your health or exposes you to some risk.

591
00:48:06,113 --> 00:48:18,222
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do it foolishly or crazily, like in the suttas, you don't go and stand in front of a mad elephant or something like that, but you certainly don't stay in a little village house because, you know, you might get sick in the forest.

592
00:48:20,583 --> 00:48:22,705
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can stay if you don't want to practice dhamma, sure, but...

593
00:48:23,553 --> 00:48:25,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, why are you a monk in the first place?

594
00:48:28,575 --> 00:48:33,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, I'm coming from, obviously, the Western point of view, where we are not brought up as Buddhists.

595
00:48:33,957 --> 00:48:42,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes people in Asian countries are ordained for traditional reasons, act like priests and stuff, and that's absolutely fine, if that's what they do.

596
00:48:42,321 --> 00:48:44,442
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm talking about somebody who does ordain,

597
00:48:46,615 --> 00:48:51,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Coming from a completely different culture, so there's absolutely no traditional value for any of the Westerners to be a Buddhist monk.

598
00:48:51,799 --> 00:48:58,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means they ordain out of some sort of inspiration based on the Buddha's teaching, based on the suttas that they read, and so on.

599
00:48:58,644 --> 00:49:07,051
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't then commit yourself to it, and set yourself, and expose yourself to some risk or something, well, you will never gonna learn.

600
00:49:07,071 --> 00:49:07,831
[SPEAKER_00]: You're never gonna learn.

601
00:49:08,332 --> 00:49:09,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Ever.

602
00:49:09,713 --> 00:49:15,057
[SPEAKER_01]: It sort of follows into the next... this dhamma is for one who's energetic, not for one who's lazy.

603
00:49:16,388 --> 00:49:21,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Here, Bhikkhu has aroused energy for abandoning unwholesome qualities and acquiring wholesome qualities.

604
00:49:22,189 --> 00:49:26,730
[SPEAKER_01]: He is strong, firm in exertion, not casting off the duty of cultivating wholesome qualities.

605
00:49:27,210 --> 00:49:36,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the dhamma is for one who's not doing... becoming a monk out of tradition, because... Out of duty or whatever.

606
00:49:37,813 --> 00:49:39,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think that might have been the case back in the day.

607
00:49:42,417 --> 00:49:43,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, dhamma is for one.

608
00:49:44,017 --> 00:49:53,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and obviously that's the reason one should do it, but I think today, Buddhism being quite a major religion part of society, I think that many other reasons people would ordain.

609
00:49:53,922 --> 00:49:54,362
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

610
00:49:54,382 --> 00:50:05,687
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not really talking about every single person who wears robes, but I'm like somebody who did ordain for relatively non-traditional reasons to practice the dhamma, and then doesn't practice it by staying

611
00:50:06,727 --> 00:50:14,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependent on the crowd, dependent on the establishment, worrying about the future, worrying about what I'll do when I get old, if I can't climb the hill or whatever else.

612
00:50:15,372 --> 00:50:22,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, instead of worrying about that, practice now, because the Buddha would say himself so many times, practice now while you can, otherwise it will be too late.

613
00:50:23,576 --> 00:50:26,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you practice now, you won't be worried about later.

614
00:50:29,319 --> 00:50:39,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the the case of Mahārāja Mahākassava, where the Buddha saw him in the forest, wearing hemp robes and living quite a hard ascetic life.

615
00:50:39,489 --> 00:50:45,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, oh, come on, you finish your work, come and stay with me, I get plenty of offers of better dwellings.

616
00:50:45,455 --> 00:50:47,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're old, you don't need to endure that pain anymore.

617
00:50:48,503 --> 00:50:55,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, well, it's not painful, because I've done all the work, I develop my mind in jhānas, I can develop them at will, establish them at will.

618
00:50:55,931 --> 00:50:59,095
[SPEAKER_00]: All this is pure joy, and then the Buddha praised that, and inferred that.

619
00:50:59,716 --> 00:51:05,562
[SPEAKER_00]: He said that would be the inspiration for future generations, hearing that.

620
00:51:07,084 --> 00:51:08,706
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not advocating carelessness,

621
00:51:10,902 --> 00:51:11,823
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not certainly...

622
00:51:12,403 --> 00:51:13,223
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not...

623
00:51:13,384 --> 00:51:15,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not endorsing cowardice.

624
00:51:15,805 --> 00:51:16,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Cowardice.

625
00:51:16,926 --> 00:51:21,729
[SPEAKER_00]: When you realize there's nothing careless about seeking solitude.

626
00:51:21,789 --> 00:51:24,110
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, it can be quite a frightening thing.

627
00:51:24,150 --> 00:51:38,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's not like a physical existential threat, people would be afraid of being alone, not having anything to do, not having duties dictating their day, what I must do, not having other people telling them what to do.

628
00:51:38,879 --> 00:51:40,120
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's the most frightening thing.

629
00:51:40,866 --> 00:51:53,618
[SPEAKER_00]: For most people, well, for any other enlightened mind, and so not becoming a monk, on one hand, and then not dealing with it on the other, well, it's a contradiction in terms.

630
00:51:55,520 --> 00:51:59,184
[SPEAKER_00]: As somebody who's inspired to practice dhamma, that's the ultimate thing you need to deal with.

631
00:52:00,645 --> 00:52:02,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning your own sense of self.

632
00:52:03,107 --> 00:52:05,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot do that for as long as you're in the crowd.

633
00:52:06,787 --> 00:52:12,355
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say crowd, I don't mean you sit in the middle of Times Square or something.

634
00:52:12,796 --> 00:52:18,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean crowded with other monks constantly, with other lay people, with other people, full stop.

